# Local development

A local run needs no external Postgres and no secrets. Leave the database variables unset and the server provisions and manages an embedded Postgres for you. Skip the signing key and it mints an ephemeral one on boot. The only thing you must configure is where artifacts go, so point storage at a local directory.

## Run it

```bash
mkdir -p .wyrd-dev/storage
export WYRD_STORAGE_URL="file://$PWD/.wyrd-dev/storage"
export WYRD_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080

mise run dev:backend
```

The server comes up on `http://localhost:8080`. Check it:

```bash
curl -s http://localhost:8080/healthz   # -> ok
curl -s http://localhost:8080/readyz     # -> readiness snapshot
```

## Get an admin key

The client-only `wyrd` CLI does not provision databases or credentials. A
fresh database has no credentials. Mint the first admin API key with the
server-owned bootstrap command, then use it as a bearer token:

```bash
mise exec -- cargo run -p wyrd-server -- init
# wyrd_sk_acme_… (shown once)
```

Wyrd does not use the standard `Authorization` header. Exchange the key for an access token, then send it as `X-Wyrd-Access-Token`:

```bash
curl -H "X-Wyrd-Access-Token: Bearer <access-token>" \
  http://localhost:8080/v1/...
```

See [Authentication](/self-hosting/authentication/) for the token exchange.

## What is development-only here

The convenience that makes local runs painless is exactly what makes this configuration unsafe to ship:

- The embedded Postgres is managed by the process, not a real cluster.
- The signing key is ephemeral. It is regenerated on each restart, so tokens stop verifying when you restart the server.
- Local disk storage is not shared across replicas.

When you move to a real environment, set `APP_ENV=production`, provide external Postgres, a real signing key, and a cloud storage backend. The production profile fails boot if any of those are missing. See [Configuration](/self-hosting/configuration/).

